{#
    The page skeleton every admin view extends.

    WHY IT EXISTS. Until story 002-002 the package rendered on two paths — the sign-in mask as a
    full Twig document, the index page as a concatenated string — and each carried its own
    doctype, stylesheet link, import map and Turbo import. Every further view of epic 002 would
    have been one more copy of the same five lines, and the first one to drift would have been
    the one nobody looked at.

    THREE VARIABLES ARE REQUIRED IN EVERY RENDER CONTEXT: `title`, `stylesheet`, `importmap`.
    `strict_variables` is on (`View\TemplateRenderer`), so a caller that forgets one gets a hard
    error instead of a page that quietly arrives without its styles — and a naked page looks
    exactly like a working one. There is no default here that would be correct. Each caller
    still assembles those three itself; a shared builder for them earns its keep once there are
    more views than can be counted on one hand, and not before.

    `importmap` IS PRINTED RAW, AND THAT IS SAFE. It is a finished `<script type="importmap">`
    element from `Asset\Importmap::scriptTag()`, JSON-encoded with `JSON_HEX_TAG`, so nothing a
    project writes into its prefix can close the element early. Escaping it here would print the
    markup instead of applying it.

    IMPORTING TURBO STARTS IT. The vendored module calls `start()` at top level, so the bare
    import is the whole wiring — every page below this layout gets Turbo navigation and
    Turbo-conformant form submits without doing anything for it.

    STIMULUS IS LOADED ONLY WHERE IT IS USED. A view declares what it needs in the optional
    `controllers` variable, a map of `{identifier: module specifier}`; the block below then calls
    `Application.start()` exactly once and registers them. A view that declares nothing — the
    sign-in mask — never loads the library at all. The specifiers are resolved through the import
    map, which is why they are bare names and not URLs. `block modules` is the way out for page
    script that is not a Stimulus controller.

    BOTH VALUES GO THROUGH `json_encode` WITH `JSON_HEX_TAG`, the same flag `Importmap` uses and
    for the same reason: an identifier or specifier containing `</script>` would otherwise end
    the element early, and these strings come from calling code, not from this file.
    `JSON_UNESCAPED_SLASHES` only keeps the specifiers readable in the page source.

    NO ADDRESS IS WRITTEN HERE. The prefix belongs to the project and the asset version to the
    package; `AdminManager::assetUrl()` and `getPrefix()` are the only places that know how the
    two become one.
#}<!doctype html>
<html lang="de">
<head>
    <meta charset="utf-8">
    <meta name="viewport" content="width=device-width, initial-scale=1">
    <title>{% block title %}{{ title }}{% endblock %}</title>
    <link rel="stylesheet" href="{{ stylesheet }}">
    {{ importmap|raw }}
{% set pageControllers = controllers|default({}) %}
    <script type="module">
        import '@hotwired/turbo';
{% if pageControllers is not empty %}
        import { Application } from '@hotwired/stimulus';

        const application = Application.start();
{% for identifier, specifier in pageControllers %}
        application.register(
            {{ identifier|json_encode(constant('JSON_HEX_TAG') b-or constant('JSON_UNESCAPED_SLASHES'))|raw }},
            (await import({{ specifier|json_encode(constant('JSON_HEX_TAG') b-or constant('JSON_UNESCAPED_SLASHES'))|raw }})).default
        );
{% endfor %}
{% endif %}
{% block modules %}{% endblock %}
    </script>
{% block head %}{% endblock %}
</head>
<body{% block bodyAttributes %}{% endblock %}>
{% block nav %}{% endblock %}
{% block content %}{% endblock %}
</body>
</html>
